WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:33.000
Hi there, Shankar here. Today we're sharing another episode of our new podcast, My Unsung Hero. We'll be occasionally dropping stories from that show into the Hidden Brain Podcast feed. To hear every story in that series, please head over to My Unsung Hero and subscribe. One of my hopes is that you'll listen to these stories before starting your day. I promise it will get you off to a good start. From Hidden Brain Media, I'm Shankar Vedantam.
大家好，我是香卡。今天我们分享新播客《无名英雄》的另一期节目。我们偶尔会在“隐藏的大脑”播客中穿插该节目的故事。要想收听该系列的所有故事，请前往《无名英雄》并订阅。我的期望之一是，大家能在开始一天的工作前听听这些故事。我保证这会让你有个美好的开始。由“隐藏的大脑”传媒制作，我是香卡·韦丹坦。

2
00:00:33.000 --> 00:01:03.000
This is my Unsung Hero. Stories where one person reached out to help another in a time of need. Hi Shankar, I would like to talk about my heroes. My unsung hero is the man in the Panama hat on the flight to Boston, as the plane you're touched out. And if I could see them today, I really wish I could thank them. I have a feeling that they have no idea how much they did for me. Today's story comes from Terry Powers.
这是《无名英雄》。讲述一个人在他人需要时伸出援手的故事。嗨，香卡，我想谈谈我的英雄。我的无名英雄是飞往波士顿的航班上戴巴拿马帽的那位男士，当时飞机……如果我今天能见到他们，我真的很希望能对他们说声谢谢。我有一种感觉，他们并不知道自己为我做了多少。今天的节目来自特里·鲍尔斯。

3
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:53.000
In 2018, just before Christmas, Terry lost her mom Durey. She was 72. A few days later, Terry went to the grocery store to pick up a few things. And I was, of course, feeling lots of emotions, but there was this numbness that prevailed over everything just so I could get through the day and take care of the tasks I needed to take care of. So I go to the checkout and the cashier greets me with how are you? And I just couldn't say fine or good and brush it off like that. I just felt I needed to say, you know what? I'm not doing great.
2018年圣诞节前夕，特里失去了母亲杜蕾。享年72岁。几天后，特里去杂货店买些东西。我当时当然百感交集，但内心有一种麻木感压倒了一切，好让我能熬过这一天，处理好我需要处理的事情。于是我走到收银台，收银员打招呼问我，你好吗？我实在说不出“很好”或“不错”然后敷衍过去。我只觉得自己必须说，你知道吗？我过得不太好。

4
00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:49.000
My mother just passed away. And she responded expressed her sympathy and we finished up the transaction. So I turned to get my bag to leave and the young man who had bag my groceries looked me right in the eye and said, was it. Just a hug. But it wasn't just a hug. This man stood there and observed and listened and then reacted and gave the one thing that is most beautiful expression of comfort
我母亲刚刚去世。她回应并表达了同情，我们完成了交易。于是我转身去拿包准备离开，那个帮我打包杂货的年轻人直视着我的眼睛说，能给我一个拥抱吗？这不仅仅是一个拥抱。这个人站在那里，观察并倾听，然后做出反应，给出了最能表达安慰和关怀的、也是他能给予另一个人的最美好的举动。

5
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:54.000
and caring that he can give another person. And I want to tell him, thank you so, so very much for making a difference for me that day with that one simple, powerful gesture. Thank you. Terry Powers of Fredericksburg, Virginia. She tells us that one of the lessons her mom taught her is that when someone is very ill, don't just ask how the sick person is, ask how the caregiver is. For more stories like Terries, be sure to listen to our new podcast, My Unsung Hero. We publish new episodes every Thursday. I'm Shankar Vedantim. See you next week. you There aren't comments yet for this episode. Click on any sentence in the transcript to leave a comment.
我想对他说，非常非常感谢你那天用那个简单而有力的举动改变了我的状态。谢谢你。特里·鲍尔斯来自弗吉尼亚州弗雷德里克斯堡。她告诉我们，她母亲教给她的道理之一是：当有人重病时，不要只问病人怎么样，还要问照顾病人的人怎么样。想听更多像特里这样的故事，请务必收听我们的新播客《无名英雄》。我们每周四发布新节目。我是香卡·韦丹坦。下周见。本集暂无评论。点击文稿中的任意句子即可发表评论。
